m came at a price  leaving afghanistan was the hardest  decision she says we're protecting her  family's identity because fatima's  brother abdul was an interpreter with  the canadian military  as the taliban advanced in august abdul  received approval to move his family  from kandahar to canada but they arrived  at the airport to this  the family waited outside the gates for  two days when they finally reached the  entrance the canadian military told  abdul there wasn't enough room on the  plane for everyone they said no we  cannot take your whole family we can  take only your  siblings so they faced a heart-wrenching  choice fatima and her son were allowed  to board but her husband and the  couple's five-year-old daughter were  forced to stay behind in afghanistan  leaving was very hard she says my  daughter was crying after me and my son  was crying after his father  fatima told herself it was the right  decision that her son was safe and  hopefully her husband and daughter would  be on the next flight but the following  day isis isis-k terrorists attacked the  airport  killing dozens grounding flights and  forcing fatima's husband and daughter  into hiding  i can't eat or sleep she says i feel  empty inside i miss my daughter and my  husband  hello  we met fatima's husband in kabul where  he and his daughter have been moving  between safe houses for three months  they applied to go to canada in august  but haven't received a response  our five-year-old is crying every day  for her mother he says the situation  here is not stable or safe for us this  was you know a delicate situation that  should have been handled appropriately  and it wasn't this canadian ex-special  forces officer was at the airport in  august as the taliban took control his  private security firm has been working  to rescue afghans with canadian visas  but those evacuations have now stalled  canada's approval process is slow and  there are few safe routes out of the  country all these families that are just  waiting now it's hard waiting and  praying the taliban doesn't find their  loved ones left behind it's my family  i worked with canadian forces and they  are paying for it jeff sample global  news kabul afghanistan  still ahead the dramatic rescue of a  woman hanging by a thread from a high  rise  some absolutely terrifying moments for  an 80-year-old woman in southern china  local media is reporting that she fell  from her 19th floor balcony while  hanging laundry her legs caught on a  clothing rack on the floor below leaving  her dangling upside down police and  firefighters came to the rescue using a  rope to lower her to safety  in western bulgaria at least 45 people  including 12 children have been killed  in an early morning bus crash the  deadliest in the country's history the  bus was carrying mostly macedonian  tourists details of what happened are  unclear some people managed to escape  out of windows and are now recovering in  hospital  breaking news from a wisconsin  courthouse a sixth person a child has  now died after a man allegedly drove an  suv through a christmas parade near  milwaukee and a warning some of the  images may be disturbing 36-year-old  daryl brooks made his first court  appearance today he's charged with five  counts of intentional homicide but a six  count is expected more charges are being  considered with over 60 people injured  on sunday a doorbell camera captured  brooks asking for help from a resident  near the parade route it also shows  police arriving and arresting him  el salvador banks on building a city  fueled by bitcoin up next why a volcano  will play a critical role  the crustaceans on christmas island are  clogging roads and causing some traffic  jams millions of red crabs are making  their way to the ocean the mass  migration happens annually as part of  the crabs mating season nowhere else in  the world does this phenomenon occur on  such a grand scale each female can  produce up to a hundred thousand eggs  the christmas island red crab is unique  to australia and is protected by law  two months after el salvador became the  first country to adopt bitcoin as legal  tender the international monetary fund  is recommending the country reverse  course citing high risk to consumer  protection and financial stability but  the central american nation is doubling  down it's planning to build an entire  city backed by bitcoin bonds and as  redmond shannon explains it involves a  volcano too  another week another silicon valley  style bitcoin event from el salvador's  young president naive bouquet  so there you go go back don't show them  guys  it may not have been as smooth as an  iphone unveiling but its ambition is  huge an income tax-free bitcoin city  focused on mining the cryptocurrency  that's been legal tender in el salvador  since september and it's going to  include everything residential areas  commercial areas services ukele says the  city designed in the shape of a coin  will cost 300 000 bitcoins to build  right now that's about 20 billion  dollars he wants to raise the cash by  issuing bitcoin bonds and says the power  intensive crypto mining will be powered  by renewable energy from an unexpected  source this is a fully fully ecological  city that's worked that works and it's  energized  by a volcano el salvador currently gets  a quarter of its power from similar  geothermal tech my impression is that he  lives in a parallel world you know  a world of illusions environmentalist  ricardo navarro says buchelli's plan  optimistically assumes the volcano could  power the energy-hungry plan  what matters is the amount of  electricity is going to demand it  here in el salvador we don't have enough  electricity produced with our own means  we have to buy oil for that the devil's  in the details i think there's a lot  that's left to be uh explained about how  bitcoin city is going to work crypto  advocate alex tapscott thinks that if  nothing else bouquet's bitcoin exploits  have grabbed headlines for a poor nation  looking for investment there are risks  but i also think there's lots of  potential and i think that the  alternative is hardly desirable for the  country bouquet and the volcano two  sources of unpredictable power on which  el salvador is betting its future  redmond shannon global news  it's a whole new world and that is  global national for this tuesday i'm  robin gill tonight your canada is this  group of elk resting in the snow near  hinton alberta we would love to see your  corner of the country so please keep  sending your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thank you for watching and i'll see you  back here tomorrow have a great night  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

